<div class="meta-item"><span class="meta-label">Version</span><span class="meta-value">1.0</span></div>
</div>
<div class="audience-banner"><p><strong>Audience:</strong> Department Web Content Managers and the District Web Team. Led by Sean A. Russell. The audit rule review continued, rule by rule: four rules reworded, thirteen moved to the Finalsite list, and a new standard for buttons on department pages.</p></div>
<nav aria-label="On this page" class="page-index"><p class="pi-label">On this page</p><ol><li><a href="#g1">The Meeting</a><span class="pi-count">2 sections</span></li><li><a href="#g2">What We Decided</a><span class="pi-count">2 sections</span></li><li><a href="#g3">Your To-Do List</a><span class="pi-count">1 section</span></li><li><a href="#g4">Resources and History</a><span class="pi-count">2 sections</span></li></ol></nav>
<h2 class="lead-heading">Summary</h2>
<div class="section-block lead-summary">
<p class="gap-note">The recording will be added here once it is posted.</p>
<p><strong>Quick fixes first.</strong> Patricia Sapp's dashboard showed two headers when she signed in to her certification page. The fix went live during the session: inside the platform the only header is now the top bar, and a page opened on its own keeps its own header. Patricia also noticed the Teams Join button looked different today; it worked once the window refreshed. Someone joined an older Hot Lab invite by mistake, so that old invite is being removed.</p>
<p><strong>How the rule review works.</strong> Each audit rule is read out loud exactly as a WCM would see it. If it makes sense on first read, raise your hand. If not, keep your hand down and say what is unclear, and the wording gets rewritten together. No judgment: like the gym, everyone is there to get better. Decisions are saved on the Audit Rule Review page, and the audit is updated from them once the review is done.</p>
<p><strong>Thirteen rules are not a WCM's job.</strong> The test: if a WCM cannot reach it and fix it in Composer, it goes to the Finalsite list. Thirteen accessibility rules failed that test. They come from Finalsite's page template, its accordion, carousel and video elements, or the AudioEye script, and they move off WCM lists. Parts of two more rules (links with no text, and text contrast) also move. That takes the review from 36 rules to about 15 left.</p>
<p><strong>Rule: no long unbroken blocks of text.</strong> The wording did not make sense on first read. Patricia Sapp asked whether it meant run-on sentences or splitting content by subject. Sean's intent: content dropped in like a novel is hard to read. Start with a short topic sentence, put the supporting details in bullets, and use accordions or tabs. Two rewrites were read out; Patricia Sapp and Ernesto Gonzalez preferred the first, "Break up long blocks of text."</p>
<p><strong>Rule: every link has readable text.</strong> The old wording described "click here" links, which is a different rule. This rule catches links with no text at all: image or icon links with no alt text, and empty links left behind after deleting text. It is reworded to say so.</p>
<p><strong>Rule: internal links use site links, not copied addresses.</strong> The steps named options that Finalsite does not have. Sean walked it live on the Academics page: right-click the link, choose Edit Link, and the dialog shows External Link, which is exactly what the rule flags. Change it to Internal Link and browse to the page. For a document, image or video, use Resources, which opens your folders directly. Ernesto Gonzalez agreed. The same wording will be used everywhere the link dialog is explained.</p>
<p><strong>Rule: "Quick Access buttons."</strong> No one on the call knew what this meant. The term came from the certification course, not from the District layout. Sean's standard: a button is not the way to display your information. Show each program's information first as regular text (about 250 characters, so people know what they are clicking through to), then a small button under it, left aligned. Keep text in the default style, with no custom sizes or styles. The reason is consistency across departments: your content should be the difference, not the layout. Early Childhood Education's Head Start and volunteer sections were reviewed as a good visual, with one fix: their program text has a custom style that needs to come off.</p>
<p><strong>Show what right looks like.</strong> New audit feature: each rule gets a "See it done right" button that opens a snapshot of a page that does it correctly, in a lightbox, so you never leave your audit.</p>
<p><strong>What is next.</strong> The review continues Thursday, November 12, and Monday, with the District Web Team reviewing in between. Sean will try to finish before Thursday. When it is done, the audit is rerun for everyone and you will be notified to run yours. The first review window ends December 18.</p>
</div>
